            <div xml:id="d490">

                <pb facs="0612" n="518" xml:id="pg518"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.001C13/83: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-12-15T11:00:00-06:00">December 15, 1929—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>378. Your 541 of Dec. 14. The Minister for Foreign Affairs informs me that his
                    Government is most grateful for the courtesy of the armed escort extended to
                    General Calles but that under Mexican laws the approval of the Senate is
                    necessary for permission to be granted armed forces to enter Mexican territory.
                    He says that there is not sufficient time for the Government to request this of
                    the Senate and that such a request in any event would create an unfavorable
                    impression on the public and would cause much embarrassment to the
                    Government.</p>
                <p>He, therefore, begs the Department to arrange for the armed escort to stop on the
                    International Bridge at Laredo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
